[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Capturing Screenshots, Page Source, and Console Logs on Failure in Selenium"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When automated tests fail, diagnosing the root cause quickly is crucial. Relying solely on assertion errors is often insufficient for complex web applications. By capturing screenshots, the raw page source (HTML), and browser console logs automatically upon test failure, you equip your debugging toolkit with comprehensive diagnostic data."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites and Setup"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial assumes you are using Java with Selenium WebDriver and a testing framework like JUnit or TestNG. Make sure you have your WebDriver instance initialized and a mechanism to detect test failures."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Capturing Screenshots on Failure"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Selenium provides the TakesScreenshot interface to capture the visible browser area as an image file. You can cast your driver instance and save the output to a designated directory."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"public void takeScreenshot(WebDriver driver, String filePath) {
    File srcFile = ((TakesScreenshot) driver).getScreenshotAs(OutputType.FILE);
    try {
        FileUtils.copyFile(srcFile, new File(filePath));
    } catch (IOException e) {
        e.printStackTrace();
    }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Capturing Page Source on Failure"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The page source gives you the exact DOM structure at the exact moment of failure. This is invaluable when elements disappear, dynamic IDs change, or asynchronous updates render the DOM differently than expected."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"public void savePageSource(WebDriver driver, String filePath) {
    String pageSource = driver.getPageSource();
    try {
        FileUtils.writeStringToFile(new File(filePath), pageSource, StandardCharsets.UTF_8);
    } catch (IOException e) {
        e.printStackTrace();
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Capturing Browser Console Logs"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Client-side JavaScript errors, failed network requests, and CORS issues often appear only in the browser console. Using Selenium's LoggingPreferences and the LogEntries API, you can extract these logs."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"public void captureConsoleLogs(WebDriver driver, String filePath) {
    LogEntries logs = driver.manage().logs().get(LogType.BROWSER);
    StringBuilder logBuilder = new StringBuilder();
    for (LogEntry entry : logs) {
        logBuilder.append(new Date(entry.getTimestamp()))
                  .append(" [")
                  .append(entry.getLevel())
                  .append("] ")
                  .append(entry.getMessage())
                  .append("\n");
    }
    try {
        FileUtils.writeStringToFile(new File(filePath), logBuilder.toString(), StandardCharsets.UTF_8);
    } catch (IOException e) {
        e.printStackTrace();
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Integrating with Test Framework Listeners"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"To automate this process, integrate these methods into a TestNG ITestListener or a JUnit execution watcher. When a test method fails, the listener catches the event and triggers all three capture methods."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"@Override
public void onTestFailure(ITestResult result) {
    Object currentClass = result.getInstance();
    WebDriver driver = ((BaseTest) currentClass).getDriver();

    String timestamp = new SimpleDateFormat("yyyyMMdd_HHmmss").format(new Date());
    String testName = result.getName();

    takeScreenshot(driver, "screenshots/" + testName + "_" + timestamp + ".png");
    savePageSource(driver, "logs/" + testName + "_" + timestamp + ".html");
    captureConsoleLogs(driver, "logs/" + testName + "_" + timestamp + "_console.log");
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use unique file naming conventions incorporating timestamps and test names to prevent overwrites."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ensure your test runner cleans up old artifacts periodically to avoid filling up storage."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Configure driver capabilities to enable logging (especially LogType.BROWSER) during driver initialization."}]
